#   1638 -r--r----- commands.c
#   4708 -r--r----- main.c
#    487 -r--r--r-- str.c
#   3631 -r--r----- subscribe.c
#   2319 -rw------- example-files/HELP
#   1552 -rw-r--r-- example-files/info-hams.info
#   1011 -rw-r--r-- example-files/packet-radio.info
#
# ============= README ==============
if test -f 'README' -a X"$1" != X"-c"; then
	echo 'x - skipping README (File already exists)'
else
echo 'x - extracting README (Text)'
sed 's/^X//' << 'SHAR_EOF' > 'README' &&
$Header: /tempf/aem/listserv/RCS/README,v 1.2 91/06/17 16:56:53 aem Exp $
$Log:	README,v $
# Revision 1.2  91/06/17  16:56:53  aem
# added installation instructions
# 
# Revision 1.1  91/06/17  15:31:52  aem
# Initial revision
# 
X
This is the UCSD listserv program, which I retrieved from ucsd.edu by
anonymous ftp.  As retrieved, it consisted of the following files:
X
Makefile
commands.c
listserv.h
main.c
str.c
subscribe.c
X
I've made a couple minor changes, to make it slightly less
UCSD-specific. I've also added this README, and example files in the
subdirectory example-files
X
INSTALLATION:
X
1) Set up the variables in listserv.h and Makefile to suit your site.
X
2) Compile the programs. Everything compiled on my MicroVax II running
ULTRIX 3.0 without any problem at all. Your results may vary.
X
3) Create your SERVDIR wherever you specified in step 1. Install a HELP
file, perhaps using the example-files/HELP in this directory as a template.
X
4) For each mailing list you will serve, create a name.info file giving
a bit of information about that mailing list. see the *.info files in
the example-files subdirectory.
X
5) Create a name file in the same directory, consisting of email
addresses one to a line of subscribers to a group. If it is for a
brand-new group, create an empty file. Remember that this file must be
writable by the mail daemon.
X
6) set up an alias in your /etc/aliases that points to this program, for
example:
X
listserv:	"|/usr/local/mail/listserv"
X
7) Depending on how you have your mailing lists set up, you may need to
add an alias to the /etc/aliases file for each of the mailing lists. For
example:
X
real-recipes: :include:/usr/local/mail/maillists/recipes
X
So sending mail to real-recipes actually goes to each of the subscribers
listed in /usr/local/mail/maillists/recipes
X
8) Install listserv in the appropriate directory.
X
9) Run newaliases to get sendmail to read the changes in /etc/aliases.
X
10) Try it out.
X
